#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/howto/coordinate-embargoed-releases.txt:5
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Content-type: text/asciidoc\n"
"Abstract: When a vulnerability is reported, we follow these guidelines to\n"
" assess the vulnerability, create and review a fix, and coordinate "
"embargoed\n"
" security releases.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title -
#: ../Documentation/howto/coordinate-embargoed-releases.txt:7
#, no-wrap
msgid "How we coordinate embargoed releases"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/howto/coordinate-embargoed-releases.txt:14
msgid ""
"To protect Git users from critical vulnerabilities, we do not just release "
"fixed versions like regular maintenance releases. Instead, we coordinate "
"releases with packagers, keeping the fixes under an embargo until the "
"release date. That way, users will have a chance to upgrade on that date, no "
"matter what Operating System or distribution they run."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title -
#: ../Documentation/howto/coordinate-embargoed-releases.txt:16
#, no-wrap
msgid "The `git-security` mailing list"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/howto/coordinate-embargoed-releases.txt:21
msgid ""
"Responsible disclosures of vulnerabilities, analysis, proposed fixes as well "
"as the orchestration of coordinated embargoed releases all happen on the "
"`git-security` mailing list at <git-security@googlegroups.com>."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/howto/coordinate-embargoed-releases.txt:27
msgid ""
"In this context, the term \"embargo\" refers to the time period that "
"information about a vulnerability is kept under wraps and only shared on a "
"need-to-know basis. This is necessary to protect Git's users from bad actors "
"who would otherwise be made aware of attack vectors that could be "
"exploited. \"Lifting the embargo\" refers to publishing the version that "
"fixes the vulnerabilities."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ~
#: ../Documentation/howto/coordinate-embargoed-releases.txt:29
#, no-wrap
msgid "Audience of the `git-security` mailing list"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/howto/coordinate-embargoed-releases.txt:34
msgid ""
"Anybody may contact the `git-security` mailing list by sending an email to "
"<git-security@googlegroups.com>, though the archive is closed to the public "
"and only accessible to subscribed members."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/howto/coordinate-embargoed-releases.txt:38
msgid ""
"There are a few dozen subscribed members: core Git developers who are "
"trusted with addressing vulnerabilities, and stakeholders (i.e. owners of "
"products affected by security vulnerabilities in Git)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/howto/coordinate-embargoed-releases.txt:44
msgid ""
"Most of the discussions revolve around assessing the severity of the "
"reported issue (including the decision whether the report is "
"security-relevant or can be redirected to the public mailing list), how to "
"remediate the issue, determining the timeline of the disclosure as well as "
"aligning priorities and requirements."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ~
#: ../Documentation/howto/coordinate-embargoed-releases.txt:46
#, no-wrap
msgid "Communications"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/howto/coordinate-embargoed-releases.txt:56
msgid ""
"If you are a stakeholder, it is a good idea to pay close attention to the "
"discussions, as pertinent information may be buried in the middle of a "
"lively conversation that might not look relevant to your interests. For "
"example, the tentative timeline might be agreed upon in the middle of "
"discussing code comment formatting in one of the patches and whether or not "
"to combine fixes for multiple, separate vulnerabilities into the same "
"embargoed release. Most mail threads are not usually structured specifically "
"to communicate agreements, assessments or timelines."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title -
#: ../Documentation/howto/coordinate-embargoed-releases.txt:58
#, no-wrap
msgid "Typical timeline"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/howto/coordinate-embargoed-releases.txt:61
msgid "A potential vulnerability is reported to the `git-security` mailing list."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/howto/coordinate-embargoed-releases.txt:65
msgid ""
"The members of the git-security list start a discussion to give an initial "
"assessment of the severity of the reported potential vulnerability.  We "
"aspire to do so within a few days."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/howto/coordinate-embargoed-releases.txt:69
msgid ""
"After discussion, if consensus is reached that it is not critical enough to "
"warrant any embargo, the reporter is redirected to the public Git mailing "
"list. This ends the reporter's interaction with the `git-security` list."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/howto/coordinate-embargoed-releases.txt:72
msgid ""
"If it is deemed critical enough for an embargo, ideas are presented on how "
"to address the vulnerability."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/howto/coordinate-embargoed-releases.txt:76
msgid ""
"Usually around that time, the Git maintainer or their delegate(s) open a "
"draft security advisory in the `git/git` repository on GitHub (see below for "
"more details)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/howto/coordinate-embargoed-releases.txt:81
msgid ""
"Code review can take place in a variety of different locations, depending on "
"context. These are: patches sent inline on the git-security list, a private "
"fork on GitHub associated with the draft security advisory, or the git/cabal "
"repository."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/howto/coordinate-embargoed-releases.txt:85
msgid ""
"Contributors working on a fix should consider beginning by sending patches "
"to the git-security list (inline with the original thread), since they are "
"accessible to all subscribers, along with the original reporter."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/howto/coordinate-embargoed-releases.txt:95
msgid ""
"Once the review has settled and everyone involved in the review agrees that "
"the patches are nearing the finish line, the Git maintainer, and others "
"determine a release date as well as the release trains that are "
"serviced. The decision regarding which versions need a backported fix is "
"based on input from the reporter, the contributor who worked on the patches, "
"and from stakeholders. Operators of hosting sites who may want to analyze "
"whether the given issue is exploited via any of the repositories they host, "
"and binary packagers who want to make sure their product gets patched "
"adequately against the vulnerability, for example, may want to give their "
"input at this stage."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/howto/coordinate-embargoed-releases.txt:101
msgid ""
"While the Git community does its best to accommodate the specific timeline "
"requests of the various binary packagers, the nature of the issue may "
"preclude a prolonged release schedule. For fixes deemed urgent, it may be in "
"the best interest of the Git users community to shorten the disclosure and "
"release timeline, and packagers may need to adapt accordingly."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/howto/coordinate-embargoed-releases.txt:103
msgid ""
"Subsequently, branches with the fixes are pushed to the git/cabal "
"repository."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/howto/coordinate-embargoed-releases.txt:105
msgid ""
"The tags are created by the Git maintainer and pushed to the same "
"repository."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/howto/coordinate-embargoed-releases.txt:109
msgid ""
"The Git for Windows, Git for macOS, BSD, Debian, etc. maintainers prepare "
"the corresponding release artifacts, based on the tags created that have "
"been prepared by the Git maintainer."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/howto/coordinate-embargoed-releases.txt:113
msgid ""
"The release artifacts prepared by various binary packagers can be made "
"available to stakeholders under embargo via a mail to the `git-security` "
"list."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/howto/coordinate-embargoed-releases.txt:118
msgid ""
"Less than a week before the release, a mail with the relevant information is "
"sent to <distros@vs.openwall.org> (see below), a list used to pre-announce "
"embargoed releases of open source projects to the stakeholders of all major "
"distributions of Linux as well as other OSes."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/howto/coordinate-embargoed-releases.txt:121
msgid ""
"Public communication is then prepared in advance of the release date. This "
"includes blog posts and mails to the Git and Git for Windows mailing lists."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/howto/coordinate-embargoed-releases.txt:125
msgid ""
"On the day of the release, at around 10am Pacific Time, the Git maintainer "
"pushes the tag and the `master` branch to the public repository, then sends "
"out an announcement mail."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/howto/coordinate-embargoed-releases.txt:129
msgid ""
"Once the tag is pushed, the Git for Windows maintainer publishes the "
"corresponding tag and creates a GitHub Release with the associated release "
"artifacts (Git for Windows installer, Portable Git, MinGit, etc)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/howto/coordinate-embargoed-releases.txt:132
msgid ""
"Git for Windows release is then announced via a mail to the public Git and "
"Git for Windows mailing lists as well as via a tweet."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/howto/coordinate-embargoed-releases.txt:135
msgid ""
"Ditto for distribution packagers for Linux and other platforms: their "
"releases are announced via their preferred channels."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/howto/coordinate-embargoed-releases.txt:139
msgid ""
"A mail to <oss-security@lists.openwall.org> (see below for details) is sent "
"as a follow-up to the <distros@vs.openwall.org> one, describing the "
"vulnerability in detail, often including a proof of concept of an exploit."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/howto/coordinate-embargoed-releases.txt:142
msgid ""
"Note: The Git project makes no guarantees about timelines, but aims to keep "
"embargoes reasonably short in the interest of keeping Git's users safe."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ~
#: ../Documentation/howto/coordinate-embargoed-releases.txt:144
#, no-wrap
msgid "Opening a Security Advisory draft"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/howto/coordinate-embargoed-releases.txt:150
msgid ""
"The first step is to https://github.com/git/git/security/advisories/new[open "
"an advisory]. Technically, this is not necessary. However, it is the most "
"convenient way to obtain the CVE number and it gives us a private repository "
"associated with it that can be used to collaborate on a fix."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ~
#: ../Documentation/howto/coordinate-embargoed-releases.txt:152
#, no-wrap
msgid "Notifying the Linux distributions"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/howto/coordinate-embargoed-releases.txt:159
msgid ""
"At most two weeks before release date, we need to send a notification to "
"<distros@vs.openwall.org>, preferably less than 7 days before the release "
"date.  This will reach most (all?) Linux distributions. See an example "
"below, and the guidelines for this mailing list at "
"https://oss-security.openwall.org/wiki/mailing-lists/distros#how-to-use-the-lists[here]."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/howto/coordinate-embargoed-releases.txt:165
msgid ""
"Once the version has been published, we send a note about that to "
"oss-security.  As an example, see "
"https://www.openwall.com/lists/oss-security/2019/12/13/1[the v2.24.1 mail]; "
"https://oss-security.openwall.org/wiki/mailing-lists/oss-security[Here] are "
"their guidelines."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/howto/coordinate-embargoed-releases.txt:170
msgid ""
"The mail to oss-security should also describe the exploit, and give credit "
"to the reporter(s): security researchers still receive too little respect "
"for the invaluable service they provide, and public credit goes a long way "
"to keep them paid by their respective organizations."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/howto/coordinate-embargoed-releases.txt:173
msgid ""
"Technically, describing any exploit can be delayed up to 7 days, but we "
"usually refrain from doing that, including it right away."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/howto/coordinate-embargoed-releases.txt:178
msgid ""
"As a courtesy we typically attach a Git bundle (as `.tar.xz` because the "
"list will drop `.bundle` attachments) in the mail to distros@ so that the "
"involved parties can take care of integrating/backporting them. This bundle "
"is typically created using a command like this:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/howto/coordinate-embargoed-releases.txt:181
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\tgit bundle create cve-xxx.bundle ^origin/master vA.B.C vD.E.F\n"
"\ttar cJvf cve-xxx.bundle.tar.xz cve-xxx.bundle\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ~
#: ../Documentation/howto/coordinate-embargoed-releases.txt:183
#, no-wrap
msgid "Example mail to distros@vs.openwall.org"
msgstr ""

#. type: delimited block .
#: ../Documentation/howto/coordinate-embargoed-releases.txt:189
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"To: distros@vs.openwall.org\n"
"Cc: git-security@googlegroups.com, <other people involved in the "
"report/fix>\n"
"Subject: [vs] Upcoming Git security fix release\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: delimited block .
#: ../Documentation/howto/coordinate-embargoed-releases.txt:191
#: ../Documentation/howto/coordinate-embargoed-releases.txt:227
#, no-wrap
msgid "Team,\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: delimited block .
#: ../Documentation/howto/coordinate-embargoed-releases.txt:197
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The Git project will release new versions on <date> at 10am Pacific Time "
"or\n"
"soon thereafter. I have attached a Git bundle (embedded in a `.tar.xz` to "
"avoid\n"
"it being dropped) which you can fetch into a clone of\n"
"https://github.com/git/git via `git fetch --tags /path/to/cve-xxx.bundle`,\n"
"containing the tags for versions <versions>.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: delimited block .
#: ../Documentation/howto/coordinate-embargoed-releases.txt:200
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"You can verify with `git tag -v <tag>` that the versions were signed by\n"
"the Git maintainer, using the same GPG key as e.g. v2.24.0.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: delimited block .
#: ../Documentation/howto/coordinate-embargoed-releases.txt:204
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Please use these tags to prepare `git` packages for your various\n"
"distributions, using the appropriate tagged versions. The added test cases\n"
"help verify the correctness.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: delimited block .
#: ../Documentation/howto/coordinate-embargoed-releases.txt:206
#, no-wrap
msgid "The addressed issues are:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: delimited block .
#: ../Documentation/howto/coordinate-embargoed-releases.txt:209
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"<list of CVEs with a short description, typically copy/pasted from Git's\n"
"release notes, usually demo exploit(s), too>\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: delimited block .
#: ../Documentation/howto/coordinate-embargoed-releases.txt:212
#: ../Documentation/howto/coordinate-embargoed-releases.txt:243
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Credit for finding the vulnerability goes to <reporter>, credit for fixing\n"
"it goes to <developer>.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: delimited block .
#: ../Documentation/howto/coordinate-embargoed-releases.txt:215
#: ../Documentation/howto/coordinate-embargoed-releases.txt:246
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Thanks,\n"
"<name>\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ~
#: ../Documentation/howto/coordinate-embargoed-releases.txt:219
#, no-wrap
msgid "Example mail to oss-security@lists.openwall.com"
msgstr ""

#. type: delimited block .
#: ../Documentation/howto/coordinate-embargoed-releases.txt:225
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"To: oss-security@lists.openwall.com\n"
"Cc: git-security@googlegroups.com, <other people involved in the "
"report/fix>\n"
"Subject: git: <copy from security advisory>\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: delimited block .
#: ../Documentation/howto/coordinate-embargoed-releases.txt:229
#, no-wrap
msgid "The Git project released new versions on <date>, addressing <CVE>.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: delimited block .
#: ../Documentation/howto/coordinate-embargoed-releases.txt:233
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"All supported platforms are affected in one way or another, and all Git\n"
"versions all the way back to <version> are affected. The fixed versions "
"are:\n"
"<versions>.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: delimited block .
#: ../Documentation/howto/coordinate-embargoed-releases.txt:235
#, no-wrap
msgid "Link to the announcement: <link to lore.kernel.org/git>\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: delimited block .
#: ../Documentation/howto/coordinate-embargoed-releases.txt:237
#, no-wrap
msgid "We highly recommend to upgrade.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: delimited block .
#: ../Documentation/howto/coordinate-embargoed-releases.txt:240
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The addressed issues are:\n"
"* <list of CVEs and their explanations, along with demo exploits>\n"
msgstr ""
